    "story": "Riding drag is a job for somebody who can stand not knowing. Ahead of her the column raises a dust she can navigate by; behind her is everything the column has decided not to watch — the road they came down, the farm they passed at noon, and whatever might be following at the pace of a grudge. She checks it the way you check a door you already locked. Not because she expects it open. Because the one time you don't is the whole story of how columns disappear.",
    "notes": "The portrait with the face withheld. Horse and rider are photographed from directly behind against a parchment-empty ground, the dapple-gray's pale flank supplying the frame's largest single mass of tone. Two diagonals do all the moving — the tail's long sweep and the rider's head turned back in profile, the composition's only concession to a face. The rear-view construction reverses the portrait contract: her sightline becomes the picture's real subject, and what it falls on is never shown. For collectors: the set's boldest viewpoint — vigilance carried entirely by posture and mass. Its pair record, chapter 63, keeps the same vigil in a second light.",
