    "story": "They hold it the way pallbearers hold — by habit, at the corners, taking the weight without being asked. The younger men call it the old flag, as if it had retired, but cloth that has been shot through in three counties doesn't retire; it gets promoted to evidence. He points at the corner where the stitching gave out at the creek crossing, telling it again for whoever will still stand still for it. The story gets longer every year. So does the list of men who can't correct it anymore.",
    "notes": "The flag, laid horizontal, becomes the picture's horizon: two old soldiers hold the worn colors stretched between them at the corners, a man at each end like the last posts of a fence, and the cloth — not any face — takes the center. One hand breaks the symmetry, pointing to the corner where the stitching gave out; it is the composition's single gesture, and it is directed at damage. The sepia register is the other decision: men, cloth, and the field behind them are unified in one tonal family, so the banner reads less as an object displayed than as a surface continuous with its keepers. Against the set's flying colors, this is the norm-break — the flag at rest, carried flat. For collectors: custody rendered as composition.",
