    "story": "Coffee is the army's real chain of command. Before the orders, before the drill, there is the cup — and a man with a full one and a crate to lean on has, for exactly as long as it stays hot, everything the war has not yet taken. He drinks it the way the veterans taught him: slow, upright, watching the camp wake up and do its arithmetic. Boots, rifles, debts, letters. When the cup is empty the day starts, and the day has never once asked his opinion. He intends to finish the cup anyway. It is the only appointment out here that he gets to keep.",
    "notes": "The tent canvas does the architecture: one great pale triangle rises behind the figure like a sail, and against it the young soldier stands at a camp crate in the portrait's single vertical. The still life is rationed to two tin cups — one in his hand, one waiting on the wood — a pair of bright accents set on the crate's long horizontal. The gaze is direct and unsmiling, the set's plainest address, and the muted palette keeps the construction in genre painting's register rather than reportage. For collectors: the interval portrait — the war's off-hours given a sail, a stage, and two cups.",
