    "story": "The book is a month old and he reads it like fresh orders. Out here news arrives the way weather used to at home — late, secondhand, and already argued about by everybody who heard it first. He reads the casualty lists the way other men read scripture: slowly, and only in daylight, and never the page with his own county on it until the coffee is poured and the ground is dry beneath him. Catching up, the men call it. What he is catching up on is a world that has agreed to go on without asking him.",
    "notes": "Heim sets the camera at the grass's own level and reclines with the subject: a sergeant stretched in dappled light, absorbed in a small book whose orange cover is the frame's single saturated note — a second, rectangular face turned up beside his own. The reclined body runs on a long diagonal, the chevrons catching the light; around it, the camp dissolves to brightness and leaf-pattern. In a set built on standing portraits, the horizontal figure is the norm-break, and the low viewpoint is its courtesy: the viewer joins the posture instead of observing it. For collectors: the war's interior life in formal terms — one diagonal, one bright rectangle, and light doing the rest.",
