    "story": "He carries the war on one shoulder, in a wooden box that weighs more than a rifle and breaks easier. The soldiers line up for him in their hundreds — clean shirt, hair wet down, the face they want sent home — and then they march out of his chemicals' reach and into the part no plate can follow. He has photographed generals who posed like statues and privates who couldn't stop shaking. The glass keeps them all at the same temperature. What he cannot decide, packing up in the dusk with the day's faces drying in their frames, is whether he is serving the living or outfitting the dead.",
