    "story": "The hand goes up the way it has gone up ten thousand times — in schoolrooms of boys who are now men, in camps where the lesson was loading, aiming, not flinching at the sound of your own gun. He teaches the way the war taught him: once, plainly, with no ornament, because ornament is what gets left out under fire anyway. The young ones watch the hand. What he's really showing them isn't in it. It's in the steadiness — the suggestion that a man can see everything he has seen and still raise a hand slowly, like there's all the time in the world. There isn't. That's the lesson.",
