    "story": "A volley is the army's way of making four hundred private decisions into one public fact. No man hears his own shot inside it — that's the mercy and the design. Load, level, wait for the word, and then the line speaks all at once and the smoke stands up in front of them like a curtain being drawn on whatever they did. Behind the curtain, other men are making the same arrangement. The officers walk the rear with their hands folded, counting nothing, watching the men's faces instead of the field. The faces tell you what the volley cost and what the next one will.",
    "notes": "Compression is the method: the ranks are stacked without depth into a single wall of dark coats, rifles crossing at every angle like a thicket, and through the center runs the picture's one bright horizontal — a muzzle flash, elongated, white-orange, gone in the instant it is drawn. Above the line at the rear center, an officer stands elevated, the composition's overseer and its only upright stillness. The faces are small, repeated, half-lost in the stack. For collectors: volley fire as pattern — a wall, a thicket, and one streak of light doing the work of a hundred shots.",
