    "story": "A column on a road through a town is an army showing its manners. Step dressed, rifles at the shoulder, the colors where the widows on the porches can see them — this is the war as it wishes to be remembered, and for the length of one main street, it gets its wish. The men know the porches are counting them. They always count. Somewhere in the column a boy is marching taller than he has ever stood in his life because an old woman has come out with a bucket of water and a look that says she has buried better than him and approves anyway. He'll remember her face at the next river crossing, when the manners come off.",
    "notes": "The companion to chapter 38's billow: here the colors hang furled and narrow at the column's center, a single vertical banner, while the ranks march out of dappled shade toward the viewer in measured recession. The fixed bayonets supply the rhythm — a picket line of thin verticals repeating into the distance — and the road's dapple does the patterning the flag declines. An officer in a slouch hat holds the front-left corner, his gaze level with the lens. For collectors: the march at its most ordered — verticals, recession, and a flag carried like a secret.",
