    "story": "The strap has chafed him raw since Tuesday, and he keeps touching it the way a man touches a sore tooth — not to fix it, just to confirm it's still his. In a moment he will stand up in front of four hundred men and tell them where to walk tomorrow, and some of them he will be naming for the last time without knowing which. He has learned to give the order the way you hand over something heavy: with both hands, looking at the man, not at the weight. First, one more breath that belongs to nobody's army.",
    "notes": "An extreme close-up, cropped inside the distance portraiture keeps. The face fills the frame on a ground of pure tone, and the composition permits itself exactly one gesture: the hand at the collar strap, placed low-left, worrying the leather — the single moving part in an otherwise monumental stillness. The palette rations its saturation to rank itself: the red of the shoulder boards and the kepi's faded red top are the only full notes, so the eye reads the insignia as color before it reads them as symbols. The mouth, caught mid-breath, breaks the era's studio convention of the sealed, timeless commander. For collectors: the officer class rendered as surface, gesture, and two rationed reds.",
