    "story": "His hands are famous in the regiment for the wrong reason: they do not shake. Not at the table with the saws laid out in their terrible order, not holding the lamp, not folding another man's hands across his chest when the work changes from saving to keeping. He has prayed with men who cursed him for it and men who begged him to start. The cross on his hat buys him passage everywhere and rest nowhere. Afterward, alone, the hands do what they want. He lets them. It's the only hour of the day that belongs to the shaking, and he has learned to schedule his grief the way the army schedules everything: briefly, and before dawn.",
    "notes": "The brightest mark in the frame is a symbol: the white cross on the tall black hat, set at the picture's top like a flag reduced to one sign. Below it the portrait is all restraint — wire spectacles, a gray beard, a dark coat with a single row of brass, one pale strap drawn on the diagonal. The gaze travels off-frame in three-quarter view, level and unhurried. Heim gives the medical service the set's plainest construction: no instruments, no wounded, only the cross, the glasses, and the man. For collectors: mercy as insignia — a portrait whose whole argument is carried by one white cross.",
