    "story": "The accounting takes longer every year, because the ledger keeps changing its rules. At first he counted the ones who didn't come home. Then he counted the ones who did and wished they hadn't, some nights. Now, sitting in the sun with his hands folded, he counts differently: the grandchildren who exist because a ball passed an inch wide of a boy in a field, the sermons preached, the fences mended, the ordinary Tuesdays that four score men he knew never got. It isn't grief anymore, exactly. It's bookkeeping for a debt the world owes the dead, and he is the last clerk still keeping it in a fair hand.",
