    "story": "The bunting is not decoration. It is the job description, draped where everyone can see it: hold this together. He stands behind it the way a man stands behind a plow or a pulpit — as the instrument, not the owner — knowing the cloth will outlast the hands and that his name will be attached to the tear if it comes. Men ask whether he sleeps. He sleeps the way a sentry sleeps, in pieces, with one part of him always on the wall where the union's seams run, listening for the sound of stitching giving way. The stars stay bound as long as he stands here. That's the whole of the arrangement, and nobody asked him if he agreed to it. He agreed anyway. That's the other part of the job.",
    "notes": "The flag, laid out as a barrier: across the frame's lower third Heim drapes the bunting in pleats — red, white, and a blue field whose stars are drawn at parade scale — and the president stands behind it, risen from the waist up like a man behind a lectern. The construction is a study in scale: the stars are the largest shapes in the picture, the man above them held in black restraint, his stovepipe hat completing the vertical. A lantern sits small at the left edge. With chapter 44's blurred foreground flag, it forms the set's pair of Lincolns. For collectors: the Union rendered as drapery — and the man bound to it by composition, standing where the cloth ends.",
