    "story": "They keep asking how he can sign again, knowing what he knows, and he has stopped correcting them. Signing was never the decision. The decision was made the first time, in a schoolhouse, by a boy who thought the beard would make him a man. Everything since has just been the terms arriving. No other choice sounds like surrender to the ones who still have choices. To him it sounds like arithmetic: the names on the roll he can still answer for, the flag that has never once asked him for anything reasonable, and a pair of boots by the door that only fit one road. He looks down so they won't see that the arithmetic doesn't frighten him. The familiarity does.",
    "notes": "Formally, the portrait is built on a refusal. Civil War command portraiture — the Brady and Gardner studios — posed its officers square to the lens, flags arranged behind them as furniture. Heim turns his sitter into profile and dissolves the flag into haze, where it survives as a single vertical band of faded red. The composition's engine is the leather cross-strap: a hard diagonal cut from shoulder to sword hilt that organizes the torso and gives the lowered head its counterweight. The palette does the same work as the pose — the uniform's blue desaturated toward gray, the flag's red weathered to rust, the national colors separated and aged in the handling itself, the union held together, chromatically, by the man standing in front of it. The light is pictorialist: soft, directionless, vignetted, declining every convention of heroic illumination. For collectors, the piece's standing is formal — a commander portrait that breaks the genre's frontal grammar and lets design, not insignia, carry the rank.",
