    "story": "Everything has a price is not cynicism, the way he practices it. It's inventory. Boots, bacon, brandy, a dry place to sleep, a letter carried to the next town, a rumor carried back — he can quote the war in any of its currencies, including the ones that don't jingle. He sits with his bag like a man at a fair that never closes, watching an army discover, item by item, what it cannot do without. The generals think they run the war on strategy. He runs his end of it on the older science: what a man will give for one more day of feeling human. The prices, he finds, are remarkably stable.",
