    "story": "In Washington they bring him the war in folders. Out here it has a smell and a sound and a woman's face behind him in the crowd, blurred by her own griefs, and he makes himself stand in it without the folder's protection. Abe, the soldiers call him when they're sure he can't hear, and Father Abraham when they think he can, and he prefers the first — it's the one that doesn't ask anything of him. He listens to a band, a speech, a silence, in that order, and files none of it. Some things a president has to carry unfiled, in the part of the mind that doesn't get briefed.",
    "notes": "The picture is an essay in differential focus. Lincoln is held tack-sharp in profile — the stovepipe hat's vertical, the beard's edge, the set jaw — while a woman's face floats behind him in the haze, rendered at the opposite end of the focus scale. Two planes, two kinds of attention: the man who must listen, and the people as atmosphere. Heim declines the frontal, monumental Lincoln of the standard iconography for a profile caught mid-audience, and the granular, plate-like surface keeps the construction honest to its period sources. For collectors: the set's essential Lincoln, and its most rigorous piece of focus engineering — sharpness itself assigned as meaning.",
