    "story": "A chair, in camp, is not furniture. It's a rank, a rumor, and a small republic of rest, and a man in possession of one sits like a magistrate — arms folded, legs crossed, jurisdiction over exactly the ground the chair covers. He has been promised relief at noon, a letter by Friday, and a war that ends by Christmas, and he believes the chair. The chair is here. The chair is real. Everything else is correspondence. When he stands up, the republic falls and the war resumes at his exact location, so he sits with the stillness of a man who knows he's holding a position. In a sense, the whole army is.",
    "notes": "The set's tonal outlier: a high-key image in a collection of mid-tones, its ground blown toward white until the camp behind the sitter survives only as ghosts. Against that bleached field, the seated soldier folds himself into the frame's single compact mass — arms tucked, legs crossed, a rough triangle of gray-blue — and the plain wooden chair becomes the picture's only architecture, its slats the one set of ruled lines in a dissolved world. The restraint is technical: exposure pushed to the edge of loss, and the figure held just back from it. For collectors, the piece is the collection's study in tonal risk — the war's quietest moment rendered at the brightest register, and held.",
