    "story": "The column takes eleven minutes to pass and she has memorized the method: start at the front, don't blink at the officers, check the left side of each file where he marches when he can. I don't see him is not despair — not yet; despair is for after the last file passes and the dust stands alone in the road. It's a report, updated every four men, to the neighbor who came with her and is doing the same arithmetic on another name. The army is a river of faces all wearing the same hat, and somewhere in it is the only face in the world. She will stand here until the river runs dry. Then she will go home and write a letter that says she is well, and ask, casually, where his regiment is posted now.",
    "notes": "The set's most rigorous construction. A young woman stands at the exact center in full focus, frontal, still — while the Union column streams past her on both sides in horizontal bands of motion blur, every soldier softened by movement, every face turned away. She is the only resolved form and the only vertical at rest in a picture built of lateral flow, so the eye returns to her the way the search itself does: involuntarily. The technique is the meaning — differential motion, one shutter speed for the army and another, in effect, for the woman watching it. Against the era's static group portraiture, the blur is the norm-break. For collectors: the collection's thesis stated in pure form — stillness against the river of men.",
