    "story": "She hears them before the dog does, most days — not the sound itself but the change in it, the way a room changes when a door opens two houses down. Guns have a grammar at this distance: the slow ones are practice, the fast ones are business, and the ones that stop all at once are the sentence nobody finishes aloud. She holds her cup and listens the way other women read letters, twice through, once for the news and once for what the news is hiding. I hear them, she tells the empty yard. It is not information. It is a vigil, kept with the ears, at the exact range where love and artillery are the same weather.",
    "notes": "A tonal norm-break: inside a color collection, the portrait is rendered in near-sepia, the set's most restricted palette, as if the image itself were listening at a distance. The construction is all direction — the face lifted and turned toward the frame's upper-left corner, the gaze exiting the picture entirely, so the composition's energy leaves with it and the viewer is left holding the stillness. The lace collar is the single bright textural accent against the dark dress, and the tin cup, low in the frame, anchors the vertical. Behind her, nothing: hazed negative space doing the work of miles. For collectors: the home front rendered as pure orientation — a portrait aimed at something outside itself.",
